**Thinking Process:**
The problem requires implementing a dual-edge triggered flip-flop that toggles on both clock edges, but since FPGAs don't support this directly, I need to break it into two separate processes. The key insight is that the output should update on both positive and negative clock edges, capturing the current data input each time. I considered using a single always block with a sensitivity list, but that's not allowed for dual-edge triggering. Instead, I'll use two separate always blocks—one for posedge clk and one for negedge clk—each updating the output based on the current d value. This approach correctly models the desired functionality while adhering to Verilog standards.

[BEGIN]
module TopModule(
  input clk,
  input d,
  output q
);

  always @(posedge clk) begin
    q <= d;
  end

  always @(negedge clk) begin
    q <= d;
  end

endmodule
